{
  "original_id": "primary-20260913-nfa-asymmetry",
  "archive_id": "BW2-PR20260913-nfa_asymmetry",
  "title": "Exact directional NFA separation for binary words with one marker",
  "batch_label": "primary",
  "kind": "result-note",
  "original_status": "unresolved",
  "scope": "Use epsilon-free NFAs over {0,1} with a finite nonempty state set, one initial state, an arbitrary accepting subset, and possibly missing transitions. Let nsep(w,x) be the minimum state count of an NFA accepting w and rejecting x, with unrestricted behavior on all other words. For every integer q>=3, the maximum of nsep(0^a 1 0^b,0^c 1 0^d), over all nonnegative integers a,b,c,d satisfying a+b=c+d, a!=c, and nsep(0^c 1 0^d,0^a 1 0^b)<=q, equals (q-1)^2+2. All word lengths are allowed and the maximum is attained. Consequently the unrestricted A(q) from source.md is at least (q-1)^2+2; its asymptotic growth remains unresolved.",
  "gate_basis": "Current released terminal, manifest, PDF, TeX freeze, snapshot files, provenance and four audits agree.",
